# Inertial automorphism lies in inertia of a place above q

Theorem statement: `ValuationSubring.exists_liesOverPrime_mem_inertiaSubgroupIn`
(`72bd3f89-9448-5182-937f-7dc173982435`), by Claude, 2026-09-05.

Proof: submission `a2590b5e-c56f-5f97-b4c6-9bbe6a5af10b`, by Claude, 2026-09-05 (ACCEPTED); locally
adapted.

Let $\overline{\mathbb Z}$ denote the integral closure of $\mathbb Z$ in $\overline{\mathbb Q} =$
`AlgebraicClosure ℚ`, and let $\mathfrak Q$ be a maximal ideal of $\overline{\mathbb Z}$. Let $q$ be
a prime number whose image in $\overline{\mathbb Z}$ lies in $\mathfrak Q$. Let $\sigma$ be a
$\mathbb Q$-algebra automorphism of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ such that for every $b \in
\overline{\mathbb Z}$ there is some $c \in \mathfrak Q$ whose image in $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ equals
$\sigma(b) - b$; that is, $\sigma$ acts trivially on $\overline{\mathbb Z}/\mathfrak Q$. The
conclusion asserts the existence of a valuation subring $A$ of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ with two
properties: first, `A.LiesOverPrime q`, meaning that the image of $q$ in $\overline{\mathbb Q}$
belongs to the nonunits of $A$, i.e. to the maximal ideal of $A$; and second, $\sigma$ belongs to
`A.inertiaSubgroupIn ℚ`, the image in $\mathrm{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb Q}/\mathbb Q)$, under the
inclusion of the decomposition subgroup of $A$ over $\mathbb Q$, of Mathlib's inertia subgroup of
$A$ over $\mathbb Q$.

This is the valuation-theoretic half of the comparison between Hilbert's ideal-theoretic
ramification theory for $\overline{\mathbb Z}$ and the `ValuationSubring` formulation of
decomposition and inertia groups: a maximal ideal $\mathfrak Q$ of $\overline{\mathbb Z}$ above $q$
gives rise to a place of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ above $q$ (the localisation of $\overline{\mathbb
Z}$ at $\mathfrak Q$, $\overline{\mathbb Z}$ being a Prüfer domain), and an automorphism inertial at
$\mathfrak Q$ is inertial at that place. It is used in the arguments bounding images of inertia and
in identifying Galois subgroups generated by inertia subgroups, which enter the Mazur step of the
Fermat argument.

